== Notes ==
* {{dlc|a+}} Some rooms contain Angel Statues that can be bombed to summon the [[Angel]]s and {{dlc|nr}} acquire key pieces.

== Trivia ==
* In the original Binding of Isaac, the windows along the walls were much larger and had different patterns resembling {{c|Isaac}} and a crucifix.
* The floor's theme, ''Everlasting Hymn'', is one of the few tracks, together with ''Morning Star'', ''Living in the Light'' and ''Revelations 13-1,'' that contains actual lyrics'': Dei, quod reliquum est, Nolite metuere, Et ossa tua liberabit, Exspectans''.
** Even though the Latin in the song likely contains a few mistakes (like ''dei'', which can only mean "of god" or "the gods" as subject of the sentence, or the inconsistency between the singular vocative "expectans" and the plural verb "nolite metuere"), the more accurate translation would be: "O thou who await, ye shan't fear what is left of God, and He will set thy bones free." Considering the fact that Isaac believes that killing himself is the morally correct thing to do (as he sees himself as being sinful or corrupted), the lyrics are encouraging Isaac to stay in the box and suffocate as his body panics for air.
* The theme of Cathedral (''Everlasting Hymn'') is noticeably shorter than other floor themed soundtracks. The second layer of the theme (for when entering a room with enemies and bosses with a high HP count) wasn't added in the official OST, with the in-game second track being barely noticeable chants. The {{s|Dark Room}}'s theme (''Devoid'') is also shorter, but actually lacks the second, intense part of the track.
* {{dlc+|r}} Interestingly, Cathedral was the only non-final level with no unique enemies. This was why it was given some enemies exclusive to the floor in ''Repentance'' (those being {{E|Holy Mulligan}}s, {{E|Holy Eye}}s, and {{E|Holy Bony|text=Holy Bonies}}).
** Holy Eyes, Holy Bonies, and Holy Mulligans were first seen in cards from the card game '''The Binding of Isaac: Four Souls'''.{{dlc-}}

